ism-requirements-au is an independent tool. It is not affiliated with, endorsed or approved by the Australian
Signals Directorate, the Department of Home Affairs or the Commonwealth of Australia. It is not an IRAP assessment
and does not provide legal advice.

The bundled knowledge base (src/ism_requirements_au/data/kb.json) contains the text of, quotations from, or identifiers
taken from the following, used under their terms:

* Australian Government Information Security Manual (September 2026), as the OSCAL catalog and baseline profiles
  v2026.09.4 published by the Australian Signals Directorate (https://github.com/AustralianCyberSecurityCentre/ism-oscal):
  control statements, revisions, update months, classification applicability and Essential Eight maturity-level tags,
  reproduced verbatim. (c) Commonwealth of Australia (Australian Signals Directorate), Creative Commons Attribution 4.0
  International.

* ASD Essentials (enterprise IT) actions, principles and domains, and the action-to-control cross-references derived
  from ASD's assessment workbook; ASD guidance publications and the IRAP and Essential Eight material, from which
  short anchored quotations are taken. (c) Commonwealth of Australia (Australian Signals Directorate), Creative Commons
  Attribution 4.0 International.

* Protective Security Policy Framework Release 2026 and Direction 002-2026 (Department of Home Affairs): obligations are
  paraphrased, with a small number of short verbatim quotations. Creative Commons Attribution 3.0 Australia.

* NIST publications (for example SP 800-218 and SP 800-161): short quotations. Works of the United States Government,
  not subject to copyright in the United States.

* OWASP (ASVS, Top 10, Proactive Controls, GenAI Security Project): identified by name and link only. OWASP material is
  CC BY-SA 4.0 and is not reproduced here.

* AEMO AESCSF v2: practice identifiers appear in control cross-references only. The licence is not stated, so no text
  is included.

Every quotation records the sha256 of its source passage and its start and end offsets, and the build that produces the
knowledge base fails if a quotation does not match its source.
